From: vincek@netcom.com (Vince Kerchner)
Subject: Extropian love

Extrobeings:

I had wanted to respond to Geoff Dale's Virtual Seminar on Extropian
Love long before this, but I've been held up by preparing for the
MacWorld Expo, etc...  Sorry.

*   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *

1.  What is an "Extropian Relationship"?  Geoff says: "any
relationship is an Extropian relationship if it is based on rational
self-determination".  The key points here seem to be (a) the
relationship is "rational", and (b) it is "self-determined".  Point
(b) seems pretty clear-cut, but what about (a)?  *Who* decides what is
rational, especially as it concerns emotional issues?  In
non-emotional contexts, I have heard "rational" defined as being
able to withstand critical evaluation by others.  However, this
definition conflicts with "self-detemined".  I don't mean to pick at
this, but perhaps a little more discussion is warranted in this area.

Geoff also says: "it is necessary to reject the limitations and
expectations of the ususal cultural definition", and later,
"two-person monogamous relationships are the cultural norm".  Does
this mean that a TPMR is *not* an Extropian relationship?  Is it
necessary for a relationship to be polygamous or "open" for it to be
Extropian?  What about multi-person monogamous relationships (e.g.
Mormon), many of which are the cultural norm for their geographic or
demographic areas?  

I think that alternatives to monogamy/polygamy must also be included
in this definition.  For example, relationships which are not based on
sex, such as those between family members, coworkers, or members of an
organization (see below) may also be Extropian.  Rather than using a
rejection of the cultural norm as a basis, I might suggest an
embracing of the Extropian Principles (v2.5), either wholely or in
part, as a criterion.  Note, however, that I would *not* include a
spiritual relationship, such as that between a Nun and the Holy Ghost
(or any of its non-Christian cultural variants) as such a
relationship, as it (and religion in general) is *not* rational, nor
is it (in the case of organized religions) self-determined by the
individual. 

2.  Is the Extropy list an Extropian relationship?  I think the answer
to this is a resounding "yes".  Recently, I have heard many of my
Extropian friends comment on how they consider other members of the
list, especially those in their own geographic areas, to be members of
their extended family, often over and above actual members of their
biological families.  It is interesting to note that most of the
people I have heard express this feeling have been women.
Furthermore, I have also heard many of my female acquaintances claim
that a fundamental psychological difference between men and women is
that women are more interested in the interconnections and
relationships between people, and in the inclusion of others into
their own social group.  I don't know if this is true for most women,
nor would I speculate on whether this phenomenon might be inate or
learned (OK, I'll speculate: it's learned).  Nevertheless, I have now
accumulated sufficient data points in this area to convince me that it
is a real sociological phenomenon.

Some list posters have made the claim that many of the psychological
differences between men and women are evolutionary in origin: i.e. men
are "hunters" while women are "child-rearers".  Personally, I think
this idea is crap (with apologies to the authors).  It seems to me
that most of the psychological and behavioral differences between the
genders, *if they really exist*, are entirely based on the ideological
memeset we are all force-fed from birth, and which is constantly being
reinforced by almost every aspect of our contemporary lives,
especially from the media.  It is not surprising to me to see that,
as these external forces are gradually removed (as they have been
during the past 25 years), that there are really very few differences
between the genders and how they react to their environment (with the
exception of points mentioned above).

--Vince



